{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261123T155317Z-0a14f25d", "ts": "2026-11-23T15:59:34Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "fix/dispositions-travel", "build": "95308783037f"}, "body": {"base_commit": "ad7aed2f24b7ad717b7b70e349d3b1fc3676470c", "base_tree": "c3546b0966c93b396628601ef9bcf811174798ca", "head_tree": "8336a213e933c11ed549aa9f404ed0d4dbae09dc", "head_commit": "43b2ab77ac1d6e767c5e352ee39a0bccacf9336c", "dispatch_commit": "43b2ab77ac1d6e767c5e352ee39a0bccacf9336c", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/issues/1207-design.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/dispositions.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/ledger.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "tests/test_dispositions.py", "tests/test_dispositions_transport.py", "tests/test_ledger_holds_no_outcomes.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/dispositions-travel.md", "documentation/issues/1207-design.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/dispositions.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/ledger.py", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "tests/test_dispositions.py", "tests/test_dispositions_transport.py", "tests/test_ledger_holds_no_outcomes.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/dispositions-travel.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Test failure in evidence: the new body-tag hash fails the text-codec policy sweep", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `prawduct-hook test-status` exits 1 and reports 1 failing test in the saved evidence (13030 passed, 1 failed): tests.test_text_codec_policy::test_every_plugin_text_write_and_encode_is_tolerant. The cause is the line this bundle added at plugin/lib/dispositions.py:660, `hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()[:12]`. `encoding_violations` treats `.encode()` with no tolerant `errors=` as a violation unless `_surrogate_free` can see the receiver statically, and it only recognises a literal or a direct `json.dumps(...)` call. Binding the dumps result to the name `canonical` hides that. The bytes really are ASCII, because json.dumps defaults to ensure_ascii, so the fix is to make that visible to the sweep: inline it as `json.dumps(body, sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\")).encode(\"utf-8\")`. Do not add an exemption. Re-run the suite through `test-evidence record`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/dispositions.py"], "failed_tests": ["tests.test_text_codec_policy::test_every_plugin_text_write_and_encode_is_tolerant"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "D6 legacy-collision notice never clears, and its remedy can be a no-op", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because the predicate in `evidence_notes.ingest` (the `diverged` set) asks 'does an incoming line share an id with a held one that has a different body?'. That is permanently true once two clones hold different `disp:R:F:1` bodies, because facts are immutable and both lines stay on the note. The question that matters is whether the collision still decides the finding's winner. So after the advised re-answer converges both clones (the new seq-2 answer wins everywhere, per D4), every later `evidence ingest`, and every `evidence sync` (which `/prawduct:pr` runs on each push), still prints '1 disposition(s) share an id ... re-answer the finding to converge' while the branch is in flight. An agent that obeys it re-answers forever, or learns to ignore the line.\n\nThe remedy has two further problems. The reason names no review or finding id, so the reader cannot tell which finding to re-answer. And re-stating the clone's own answer is refused as `unchanged` by `record`: the winner it compares against is its own legacy fact, so the natural re-answer writes nothing and converges nothing. `test_ingest_counts_one_collision_once` pins only the firing case. No test re-answers and then ingests.\n\nFix: count a collision only while no answer to that finding outranks the colliding id under `answer_key`, and name `review_id/fid` in the reason. Make the advice say a NEW answer is needed, or let `record` mint a new answer when the winner is a collided legacy id. Pin it with a test that re-answers, re-ingests in both clones, and asserts the count is gone, with the un-answered case as the control. (hook-surface rule: \"Prose that REPLACES a deleted control is load-bearing logic: pin every rendered branch\"; authoring rule: \"A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition\".)", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_dispositions_transport.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "answer_key raises on an oversized sequence, against its 'never raises' claim, and consolidation does not catch it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `_id_seq` (plugin/lib/dispositions.py) runs `int(head)` whenever `head.isascii() and head.isdecimal()`. On Python 3.11 and later (this machine runs 3.14), an ASCII digit run longer than 4300 digits raises `ValueError: Exceeds the limit (4300 digits) for integer string conversion`, which I checked directly. Disposition ids come from notes fetched off the remote (`_parse` checks only that `id` is a non-empty string) and from a possibly hand-edited local store. So one such line makes `disposition_index` raise in every clone that ingests it. That breaks the census and briefing, `stats`/`review-stats`, `auto_accept`, `record`, and `answered_lint_results`, which `record_lint_partial` calls with no guard, so consolidation itself fails. Only `prior_dispositions` sits behind a `ValueError` catch. This contradicts the docstring's 'this never raises' and the security-model norm that malformed governance state fails soft. Fix: bound the digit run before converting (any id with more digits than a real sequence could reach parses as seq 0), and add an `answer_key` test with a 5000-digit id that asserts `(0, id)`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/dispositions.py", "tests/test_dispositions.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The legacy-collision report never clears once its remedy is applied, and names no finding", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the report's predicate is a historical fact (a held disposition id whose incoming body differs), while its remedy changes a different state (the finding's winner), so obeying the remedy cannot make the predicate false. In evidence_notes.ingest, `diverged` is built from `held_answers[id] != record['body']` on every ingest. After the builder re-answers as told, the new tagged answer mints max+1 and wins in both clones, but both legacy lines are still held with different bodies. So every later `evidence ingest`, `evidence sync` and session-start ingest over the branch's span prints the same count and the same 're-answer the finding to converge'. tests/test_dispositions_transport.py::TestALegacyIdCollisionIsReported even pins the repeat (`again = _fetch_and_ingest(b)` still reports it), and no test re-answers and then re-ingests. The message also gives only a count. It names no id or finding, so the agent told to 're-answer the finding' must dig the store to learn which one. This is the authoring rule 'A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition \u2014 non-terminating advice gets obeyed'. Fix: drop a diverged id from the count when the store holds an answer to the same (review_id, fid) whose `dispositions.answer_key` seq is above the colliding id's, meaning the finding was re-answered and every clone now agrees. Name the remaining ids, or their review/finding pairs, in the reason. Better still, return the diverged ids from ingest and let dispositions, which owns answer_key, decide which still diverge, so the transport module stops interpreting disposition ids. Add a test that collides, re-answers, syncs both clones, and sees no report.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_dispositions_transport.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "governance-telemetry.md's `stats` section still says only review and resolution facts arrive from other clones", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: Chunk 03's falsifying search changed the doc's line-17 statement of the claim but missed the same claim worded differently in the `stats` section. plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md:405 reads 'The store also holds the review and resolution facts ingested from other clones' notes (`refs/notes/prawduct`), so two clones of one product count those reviews in both.' Dispositions are now ingested too, and `stats` reads a finding's outcome from them (`stats.finding_outcome`; D8 says acted-on rates now count the answer). A maintainer comparing two clones' `stats` output would wrongly treat outcome rates as local to each clone. This is the core rule 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees: search for the claim, not the tokens you edited'. Remedy: make the sentence relational rather than re-enumerating, e.g. 'the transported facts (`evidence.TRANSPORTED_KINDS`) ingested from other clones' notes \u2026 so two clones count those reviews, and the answers to them, in both'. A grep for 'review and resolution' near ingest/notes/clone over plugin/ and documentation/ found no other site.", "files": ["plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The legacy-id collision notice can never clear, and it does not name the finding its remedy asks you to re-answer", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the predicate in `evidence_notes.ingest` (an incoming disposition whose `(kind, id)` is already held with a different body) looks at the two immutable colliding lines, and those lines stay in both the store and the merged notes forever. Re-answering the finding, which is the remedy the reason gives ('re-answer the finding to converge'), mints a new tagged id at seq+1. That makes the winners converge but leaves the predicate true. So every bounded `evidence ingest` and `evidence sync` in both clones repeats '1 disposition(s) share an id ...' for as long as the anchor is in range, with `--all` included. The agent following the advice re-answers, sees the same line again and re-answers again, adding a disposition each time. The notice also carries only a count, so nobody can tell which finding to re-answer. `TestALegacyIdCollisionIsReported.test_ingest_counts_one_collision_once` asserts the repeat ('again') and never checks a re-answered finding. Fix: count a collision only while the colliding id is still its finding's winner, i.e. `dispositions.disposition_index(known)[target]['id'] == record['id']`. Name the `review_id/fid` in the notice. Add a test where B re-answers and the notice disappears in both clones. Cites the learnings rule 'A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition'.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_dispositions_transport.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: the private-names scan before dispositions first reach the public remote depends on someone remembering it, and its step names a listing no command produces", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the core.md rule 'Before the first write to a shared surface (push, issue, PR, comment, artifact) after reading private sources, check its visibility and grep the payload for their names' is written down, and no hook, gate or `record_lint` check owns it. This change depends on it at a moment nothing announces. The design (`documentation/issues/1207-design.md` \u00a7 Security model and norms) measures 6 dispositions in this store that name another governed product, and says this repo's remote is public. It then hands off to an 'Operator step, not code: before the first sync from this repo after the change ships, list what `reconcile` would attach and grep it'. The build plan's Governance Checkpoints (oracle) repeat it. No CLI lists what `reconcile` would attach: `evidence status` prints only a count, and `unattached()` is reachable only from Python. The step's only homes are a design doc and a plan that is archived at merge, while the sync it must come before runs automatically inside `/prawduct:pr` on whatever branch is next. Also, `record` now attaches every new disposition to the local ref, and the next sync pushes that ref whole. So the scan is most likely skipped, and free-text reasons naming private products go to a public remote. Closing the class takes a construction rather than a reminder: a payload check that the push path runs before it pushes (outbound note lines grepped against a configured or derived list of private names), or at least a read-only listing op for unattached facts that the sync step consults. Until then, put this instance's scan somewhere that is read at the moment it is due.", "files": ["documentation/issues/1207-design.md"], "check": "rule-unenforced", "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md:22", "recommendation": "architecture carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 7 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-8"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Two other descriptions still say a fact whose note failed stays in this clone", "recommendation": "The integration departure made `evidence_notes.unwritten_notice` the one owner of the sentence: such a fact is attached by the next `evidence sync` while its branch is in flight. Two carriers of the old claim survive. One is the `_cmd_plan_import_status` docstring in plugin/bin/prawduct-hook (around line 8908): 'a fact whose note could not be written is named in a NOTE, since that one stays in this clone'. The other is plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md item 23: 'that one stays in this clone, so keep the block until it is resolved'. The doctor instruction errs on the safe side (it keeps the block), so following it does no harm. Delete or relativise the claim rather than reword it.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The budget sweep's NOTE is a second wording of the reach-repair claim the plan gave one owner", "recommendation": "The plan's mid-build decision says the 'note not written' sentence 'now has one owner, `evidence_notes.unwritten_notice`'. `_refuse_over_budget` composes its own: '`prawduct-hook evidence sync` attaches them before it pushes'. That wording is unconditional, while the owner bounds the repair to 'while its branch is in flight'. Both hold for in-scope sweep reviews today. If the reconcile bound changes, though, only one of them gets edited. Route the line through a count-taking form of the shared notice, or have it name the owner's bound.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "unwritten_notice's 'its branch' is the answered review's branch for a disposition, not the branch it was recorded on", "recommendation": "`unattached` bounds a disposition by its review's anchor, so a failed attach answering a review on a merged or other branch is never reconciled from the current one. The notice's 'while its branch is in flight' reads naturally as the branch the agent is on. The case is rare, and the wording is technically true if 'its' means the review's. Say 'its review's branch' for the disposition caller if this ever bites.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The `unsent` coercion in `_refuse_over_budget` defends against a shape `auto_accept` never returns", "recommendation": "`auto_accept` always returns an int `unsent` on 'swept'. The isinstance guard, the `{**swept, 'unsent': ...}` rewrite and the test pinning an `unsent`-less swept dict defend a hypothetical. Per the plan, nothing reads `swept['unsent']` except that test. `swept.get('unsent', 0)` alone feeding the note would do.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_dispositions_transport.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The transport module now carries disposition-body knowledge that dispositions.py owns", "recommendation": "`evidence_notes.unattached` re-extracts `body['finding']['review_id']` with its own guard. `dispositions._target`'s docstring calls itself the 'one guarded reader for every site that joins on a finding'. `ingest` also interprets same-id/different-body dispositions as 'the untagged legacy id' without checking the id is untagged. A tag collision is improbable, so the message is near-true, but the knowledge sits in the generic transport. If the finding body or id shape changes, these sites drift silently. Consider an anchor-of(fact) hook the kind owner supplies. This folds with the first finding's recommendation to return diverged ids to dispositions.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "tests/test_dispositions_transport.py copies the sterile-git fixtures verbatim", "recommendation": "Scope: class. `_sterile_git`, `_git` and `_commit` are byte-identical to tests/test_evidence_sync.py, and `_sterile_git` also lives in tests/test_evidence_notes.py. ~48 test files set GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL themselves. A shared conftest fixture would be the one owner, so a new leaking env var gets fixed once. This is a pre-existing pattern that the new file extends. Not owed in this change.", "files": ["tests/test_dispositions_transport.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "review-cycle.md's edited bullet has 'its tree' with a plural antecedent", "recommendation": "'Review, resolution and disposition facts \u2026 A clone lacking its tree reads that interval as a gap' replaced 'a fact's tree'. The meaning is still recoverable. 'a fact's tree' was clearer. No edit is wanted unless the line is touched again.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "1207-design.md D6 still quotes the pre-integration message wording", "recommendation": "D6 quotes 'recorded here before #1207', while the code says 'under the untagged legacy id'. The plan's Decisions made mid-build records the departure, and the test matches a prefix common to both. Inert. Nothing reads the quote.", "files": ["documentation/issues/1207-design.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "title": "The security model's inbound reach list leaves out two readers an ingested disposition reaches", "recommendation": "`security-model.md` (oracle) says an ingested disposition reaches 'the census, the briefing and the reviewer's prior-dispositions block, and no gate'. It also reaches `critic_consolidate.answered_lint_results`, where a forged or carried ACCEPT/FILE stops a record-lint WARNING from being re-raised in this clone's reviews, and `auto_accept`'s skip set. The design's D8 table lists both. The 'cannot clear a blocking finding' claim holds, so this clears no Records Pass bar. Recorded so the trust surface is read as the D8 table states it, not as the three-item list.", "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "unwritten_notice's 'while its branch is in flight' is ambiguous for a disposition answering another branch's review", "recommendation": "`unattached` counts a disposition as missing only on the branch where its answered review is anchored. When a disposition is recorded on branch X for a review of branch Y and its attach fails, a sync on X does not attach it. Only a sync on Y does. The NOTE's 'its branch' reads as the branch the fact was written on. This case is rare and the message is advice, so it is noted rather than rated. A relational wording ('the branch of the review it answers') would make it exact.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Session-start ingest drops ingest's non-error reason", "recommendation": "`_session_start_ingest` prints `reason` only when `status != ok`. So the legacy-collision count and the 'of a kind not transported' count reach the agent only through a manual `evidence ingest` or `evidence sync` (both print it, pinned by `test_evidence_ingest_and_evidence_sync_print_it`). That is acceptable since sync is the path `/prawduct:pr` runs. Noted in case session start is meant to be where a cross-clone divergence first shows up.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "title": "Records Pass coverage", "recommendation": "Rated the full files_oracle set against the three bars: `.prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md`, `.prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md`, `.prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md`, `.prawduct/changes/dispositions-travel.md`. I checked these claims against the code: the D4/D5 id and winner rule, the deterministic tag body, the closed list of untransported kinds (equal to KNOWN_KINDS minus TRANSPORTED_KINDS), the four newly published fields (equal to the disposition body), the plan's mid-build decision that `critic-begin` prints the refusal's `notes` (prawduct-hook prints them as PRAWDUCT NOTE), and the change fragment's 'no gate reads a disposition' (finding_state maps no disposition into RESOLVED_STATES). Apart from the plan's operator-scan checkpoint, which is covered by the rule-unenforced finding on its subject twin in the design doc, none clears a bar. The governed-by-gap is record-lint's and is not restated.", "oid": "O-12"}], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": "dispositions-travel", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03"], "base_reviewed": "ad7aed2f24b7ad717b7b70e349d3b1fc3676470c", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/dispositions-travel.md", "documentation/issues/1207-design.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02", "03"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md", "findings": [{"check": "governed-by-gap", "path": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-dispositions-travel.md", "line": 22, "detail": "architecture carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 7 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one"}], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 1, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-23T15:53:22Z"}}

